{
 "name": "loop_slice_ring_walk_divergence",
 "description": "How the loop-slice ring walk differs from the reference's, in two unrelated ways. (a) A ring that runs into a TRIANGLE: the reference cuts the quad half of a quad+triangle base at 0.5 and re-rings the triangle through the new vertex on the shared edge (5 vertices -> 7, 2 faces -> 3); our walk stops and the command is rejected, so nothing is cut. That half is a CAPABILITY GAP -- closing it means teaching the walk to terminate into a non-quad, not adjusting a parameter. (b) Three cuts across a 3x3 grid from one seed edge: the two engines produce THE SAME 28 VERTICES, THE SAME 45 EDGES AND THE SAME 18 FACES, and still disagree about 8 of those 18 face rings. Both cut the same band at the same three parameters; on our side the cut order along alternate rails is reversed, so a quad joins parameter t on one rail to 1-t on the next and the four quads of a column overlap each other instead of tiling it. The cut set {0.25, 0.5, 0.75} is its own mirror image, which is exactly why the vertex channel cannot see this.",
 "source": "Reference side: headless command-port capture of the loop-slice tool (dirty-geometry sweep, task 1123, cases loop_mid_triquad at count 1 / pos 0.5, and loop_three_grid33 at count 3). vibe3d side: live drive of this build.",
 "provenance": {
  "schema": 1,
  "source": "live-capture",
  "reference": "ref-editor@11.2v3",
  "method": "command",
  "captured_utc": "2026-08-17",
  "harness": "tools/local/capture/dirty (dirty-geometry x tool sweep, task 1123)",
  "task": "1140",
  "notes": "INSTRUMENT: the reference side is a BEHAVIOURAL CAPTURE driven through the reference editor's own headless command port -- no display, no pointer, no debugger and no static read of its binary; every number in `reference` is a coordinate the reference itself printed after running the operation on the mesh in `input`. The vibe3d side is a LIVE DRIVE of this build over the HTTP test API through the very steps this fixture stores, read back from /api/model. Both engines are given the SAME base mesh (the reference's own dump, injected verbatim) and the selection travels by COORDINATE, so no element index is assumed to mean the same thing on both sides. Nothing here is an inference about the reference's internals; where a MECHANISM is guessed at, the case note says so in that word."
 },
